/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A rotary incremental encoder is a disc with N slots at equal angles, spinning between a light-emitting diode (LED) and a photodetector.
   - A second track B has windows a quarter of a slot pitch ahead of track A, so B leads A by 90° of the pulse cycle.
   - A linear scale works the same way.
   - p is grating pitch in µm on a linear scale (or slot pitch in degrees on a rotary disc); the answer carries the same unit as p, per count: µm per count for a linear scale, degrees per count for a rotary one.
   - Channels A and B are two square waves 90° apart.
   - Read downwards, the table shows B leading A and the counter adds one.
   - N is slots per revolution; 4 is the four edges per slot.
   - resolution is the smallest angle one count represents, in degrees; N is slots per revolution; 360° is one full turn.
   - An absolute encoder has n concentric tracks on one disc, each read by its own LED and photodetector.
   - Plain binary code has a flaw.
*/
