/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A tachogenerator is a small machine coupled to the shaft.
   - E is the generated emf in volts (V); ω is the angular speed in rad/s; K is the machine constant in V·s/rad.
   - The DC output ripples at fripple = S n / 60, with S commutator segments and n the speed in rpm.
   - Loading is the main error: the meter draws current I and the armature resistance Ra drops V = E − I Ra, so a low-impedance meter reads low.
   - An AC tachogenerator has no commutator or brushes.
   - A stroboscope is a xenon tube flashing at a rate f set by an oscillator.
   - f is the flash rate in flashes per second (Hz); z is the number of marks painted on the shaft; n is the true speed in rpm; k = 1, 2, 3, … The image looks still whenever this holds.
   - At k = 1 the dial reads the true speed.
   - Set the flash rate high, well above the expected speed, and lower it.
   - Uses: calibrating tachometers, checking fan blades, watching a loudspeaker cone, inspecting printing registration.
*/
